{
  "title": "Mixed-number ranges corrupt ingredient amounts",
  "description": "During a pipeline snapshot audit on 2026-04-22, the Half \
Baked Harvest wrap snapshot showed that mixed-number ranges can corrupt both \
the parsed amount and the parsed item name.\n\
\n\
Concrete example:\n\
\n\
  - \
`data/pipeline-snapshots/halfbakedharvest-com_pesto-chicken-and-avocado-bacon-salad-wraps.json`\n\
    Cached HTML: \
`~/.ramekin/http-cache/halfbakedharvest-com_pesto-chicken-and-avocado-bacon-salad-wraps/response.bin`\n\
    Source ingredient from the embedded recipe data: `1-1 1/2 cups grilled \
chicken, cubed or shredded`\n\
    Snapshot output:\n\
      - `raw`: `1-1 1/2 cups grilled chicken, cubed or shredded`\n\
      - `item`: `1/2 cups grilled chicken`\n\
      - `measurements`: `[ { \"amount\": \"1-1\", \"unit\": null } ]`\n\
\n\
The mixed-number range should stay attached to the main cup quantity, but the \
pipeline splits it at the wrong boundary and leaves a numeric fragment at the \
front of `item`. This produces junk structured data even though the source \
ingredient is clear and valid.\n\
\n\
We should make the amount parser treat forms like `1-1 1/2 cups`, `1 1/2-2 \
cups`, and similar mixed-number ranges as a single amount expression instead \
of truncating the leading number and spilling the remainder into `item`.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
  "type": "bug",
  "labels": [
    "pipeline",
    "ingredient-parser",
  ],
  "created_at": "2026-04-22T14:33:50-07:00",
